WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.121 --> 00:00:02.220
(mellow music)

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.350
- [Robert] Before we even
started the design process,

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.510
I showed Sue some diagrams.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.450
One was built into the hillside,

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.580
but it was contrasted
with the second option

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.130
which was more of the tree-house version.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.780
Sue knew exactly what she wanted.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.340
- The way I survived the first weeks

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.290
after my husband's death was to be

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.940
up in the trees of Great Falls.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.190
So when I was ready to
move out of the house

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.890
where Mike died, what I
immediately looked for

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.350
was a lot that was full of trees.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.090
So I could get that feeling,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.890
that feeling of being in nature,

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.769
which was so critical to my
healing in those early days.

00:00:37.769 --> 00:00:40.352
(mellow music)

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.850
- The first thing we did is
to look carefully at the site,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.160
at the various experiences
that were possible.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.080
And that meant thinking
three-dimensionally,

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.520
not just about the ground plane,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.020
not just about where you're
walking on the ground,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.823
but what's up in the air.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.700
- Welcome to my Tree-house.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.423
This place is my haven, my
oasis, and my work of art.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.280
The experience walking into my home starts

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.890
whenever you walk across
a floating walkway.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.930
- [Robert] That bridge-like entry is

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.330
the first kind of moment
of leaving the land,

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.360
and you're also seeing
the nature below that,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.540
almost like you're on a
boardwalk in a nature preserve.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.040
- This is my Great Room.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.900
One of the beauties of
this room is you can see

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.320
front-to-back and side-to-side
the trees everywhere,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.390
and also have this beautiful fireplace

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.761
and my poet's corner all
combined into this room.

00:01:36.761 --> 00:01:37.594
(uplifting music)

00:01:37.594 --> 00:01:39.360
What I love about this corner is

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.180
that you can take a look
at the entire viewshed.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.970
So that's a term that Robert,
my architect, taught me

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.360
where you're taking a look at
the open space ahead of you.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.030
- She was very clear
about what she wanted,

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.250
but she never said what style.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.640
And to me, that is actually
the essence of modern.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.900
It's not about style.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.140
It's about addressing the
actual needs that you have.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.490
And that's a form of freedom,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.950
and that's a form of liberation.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.660
- This is my screen porch

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.790
and this is a room that
I spend so much time in

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:10.470
all weekend long.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.540
These screens are next level.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:13.680
They're floor to ceiling,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.980
with only a bifurcation in the
middle for safety purposes,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.140
they let you feel like you're outside.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.420
So this room is a very much of a draw

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.530
for when people come over.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.020
They want to spend time
on the screen porch.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.580
- I think on the screen porch

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.290
more so than anywhere else in the house,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.710
you feel in the trees, you
don't have that layer of glass.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.480
It is a little bit dangerous,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.240
because you're up so
high, but you're safe.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.540
That's by design (laughs).

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.030
- All right, here we are in the kitchen.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.010
So the most beautiful
feature of this kitchen

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:44.970
are these windows.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.460
These are spectacular, the
way that they wrap around,

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:48.390
and you can get a view

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.880
of all the different
kinds of trees outside.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.320
And then on a personal note,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.210
one of the parts of the
kitchen that I love the most

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.410
is the sink.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.380
So Mike and I, my husband,
at a prior house had

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.300
a beautiful sink, this Kallista sink.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.580
And so whenever we were
designing this kitchen,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.980
I wanted to incorporate a
little piece of the past

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.290
into the present and the future.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.300
- Because we wanted to take
advantage of that view,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.190
there are no upper
cabinets in this kitchen,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.130
so we had to think about
how to make it functional.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.670
And we solved that by creating
a wall of vertical cabinets

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.329
that have the refrigerator
and the pantries,

00:03:20.329 --> 00:03:23.070
basically all the storage in one element,

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.920
that's very convenient to the island.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.330
- My true favorite room
in my house is my office.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.220
One of the biggest ways that I healed

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.290
after the loss of my husband was reading.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.487
Right out of the gate I read the classic,

00:03:36.487 --> 00:03:38.640
"A Year of Magical
Thinking" by Joan Didion,

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.270
and many others that told
stories about grief and loss.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.070
So sometimes when I'm on a work call,

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.230
I'll just climb up on my
couch and pull out a book

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.820
that I think is appropriate for the person

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.320
I'm talking to on a call.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.900
I'll remember something, an
insight that I gained from one,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.850
and I'll want to pull it out for someone.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.630
Oh my gosh, Dave Grohl the
most hilarious, awesome book

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.130
but full of life lessons,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.710
because people think that
nobody talks about loss,

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.717
but every single memoir I have here

00:04:03.717 --> 00:04:06.930
has an example of how someone
has gotten through loss.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.227
For Dave Grohl, that was
his Nirvana experience.

00:04:10.227 --> 00:04:12.660
(mellow guitar music)

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.260
Here on the master bedroom
level, you get to see the canopy.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.840
So that's why I refer to
this floor as my bird's nest.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.080
I'm a bird tucked in the
very top of the trees.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.880
Of course, we get to have the feeling

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.270
of a floor to ceiling window
consuming the entire room.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.860
But when you look at it in reality,

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.840
there is a space on either side.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.300
So that space is designed
to make the windows

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.290
more economical,

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.600
so that the window company can make them

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.250
more in their normal thresholds,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.860
rather than it being completely custom.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.170
- There's a benefit, cost
benefit, that we always look at.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.915
So that extra inch, is it
really worth, you know, $5,000?

00:04:46.915 --> 00:04:47.820
Probably not.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.140
- And then also you'll notice

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.030
there are no shades on these windows.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.190
I'm so high up, no one's seeing into them.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.080
And in addition, it
just makes me feel like

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.310
I'm more in nature.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.620
I'm an early riser, so
I get up with the sun,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.230
sometimes from my bed I watch the sun rise

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.063
through the windows,

00:05:02.063 --> 00:05:03.840
and watch all the colors of the sky,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.508
and that's the way I like it.

00:05:05.508 --> 00:05:08.100
(uplifting music)

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.110
When it came to designing this space here

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.580
in the master bath,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.700
I had lived 18-years of
marriage with double sinks.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.227
So I kept telling Robert and Ben,

00:05:16.227 --> 00:05:18.360
"No, we'll put two sinks in here."

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.900
And they kept looking at me
very nicely and very kindly,

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.250
and didn't wanna point out

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:24.630
that I was just a single person,

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.460
and what would I need a double sink for?

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.960
So we had a come-to-Jesus moment

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.548
where we were staring at the
plans at their New York office,

00:05:30.548 --> 00:05:33.840
and I finally had to
really absorb the fact

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.843
that I was a single person and
a single sink would be great.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:42.270
Is this style of architecture

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.310
what Mike Deagle would've wanted?

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:45.960
No, but that's kind of the beauty of it.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.867
I get to express myself in it

00:05:47.867 --> 00:05:50.580
while knowing that whatever it would take

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.410
for us to heal and move forward,

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.369
whatever it would take
to support our lives,

00:05:54.369 --> 00:05:56.103
he would support.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.900
One really precious thing
to me is this letter

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.720
that my husband wrote me 10-years almost

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.167
before he died.

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:07.357
"Sweetheart, I was just thinking about

00:06:07.357 --> 00:06:09.577
"how much I missed you.
(melancholy music)

00:06:09.577 --> 00:06:12.367
"Sometimes when I fly, I
think about what would happen

00:06:12.367 --> 00:06:15.427
"if I passed away and
you, Connor and Kendall

00:06:15.427 --> 00:06:16.837
"were without me.

00:06:16.837 --> 00:06:19.327
"I wonder, will they be okay?

00:06:19.327 --> 00:06:22.087
"Have I done all I can
so that they will be?

00:06:22.087 --> 00:06:24.063
"If indeed something should happen,

00:06:24.063 --> 00:06:26.917
"know that all I would want for you all

00:06:26.917 --> 00:06:29.137
"is to move forward with your lives,

00:06:29.137 --> 00:06:31.597
"living them fully and happily.

00:06:31.597 --> 00:06:33.687
"Your devoted husband, Michael."

00:06:35.203 --> 00:06:36.036
Oh, look!

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.763
(mellow music)

